### What is thenApply?

thenApply transforms the result of a CompletableFuture synchronously. The function receives the completed value and returns a new value. It is the equivalent of `map()` in streams.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ThenApplyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CompletableFuture<String> balanceStr = CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> "150000.75");

        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> balance = balanceStr
            .thenApply(s -> new BigDecimal(s)); // sync: String to BigDecimal

        CompletableFuture<String> formatted = balance
            .thenApply(b -> "Balance: Rs." + b); // sync: BigDecimal to String

        System.out.println(formatted.join());
    }
}
```

```text
Balance: Rs.150000.75
```

### What is thenCompose?

thenCompose chains another async operation. The function receives the completed value and returns a new CompletableFuture. thenCompose flattens the result so you get `CF<U>` instead of `CF<CF<U>>`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

public class ThenComposeDemo {
    static CompletableFuture<Integer> fetchCreditScoreAsync(String accountId) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
            // Simulate async API call to credit bureau
            return 750;
        });
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CompletableFuture<Integer> score = CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> "SAV2001")                      // CF<String>
            .thenCompose(id -> fetchCreditScoreAsync(id));     // CF<Integer>
        // thenCompose flattens: CF<CF<Integer>> becomes CF<Integer>

        System.out.println("Credit score: " + score.join());
    }
}
```

```text
Credit score: 750
```

### What is thenCombine?

thenCombine merges two independent futures. Both run in parallel, and the BiFunction runs when both complete.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ThenCombineDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> rajeshBalance = CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> new BigDecimal("150000"));

        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> priyaBalance = CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> new BigDecimal("250000"));

        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> total = rajeshBalance
            .thenCombine(priyaBalance, BigDecimal::add);

        System.out.println("Total: Rs." + total.join());
    }
}
```

```text
Total: Rs.400000
```

### How do thenApply, thenCompose, and thenCombine compare?

| Method | Input | Function returns | Result | Stream equivalent |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| thenApply | CF<T> | U (plain value) | CF<U> | map() |
| thenCompose | CF<T> | CF<U> (future) | CF<U> | flatMap() |
| thenCombine | CF<T> + CF<U> | V (plain value) | CF<V> | (no equivalent) |

### How do you build a full async pipeline?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FullPipeline {
    static CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> fetchBalanceAsync(String id) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> new BigDecimal("150000"));
    }

    static CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> fetchTaxRateAsync() {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> new BigDecimal("0.10"));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal interestRate = new BigDecimal("0.065");

        CompletableFuture<String> result = CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> "SAV2001")                           // get account ID
            .thenCompose(id -> fetchBalanceAsync(id))                // async: fetch balance
            .thenApply(bal -> bal.multiply(interestRate))            // sync: calculate interest
            .thenCombine(fetchTaxRateAsync(), (interest, tax) ->
                interest.subtract(interest.multiply(tax)))           // combine with tax
            .thenApply(net -> "Net interest: Rs." + net)            // sync: format
            .exceptionally(ex -> "Error: " + ex.getMessage());      // error handling

        System.out.println(result.join());
    }
}
```

```text
Net interest: Rs.8775.000
```

### What about async variants?

Each method has an async variant that runs the function on a separate thread instead of the completing thread.

```java
cf.thenApply(fn);            // fn runs on completing thread
cf.thenApplyAsync(fn);       // fn runs on ForkJoinPool thread
cf.thenApplyAsync(fn, pool); // fn runs on custom pool
```

Use non-async for cheap transforms (parseInt, string format). Use async for heavy or blocking work.

### How does error handling work in the pipeline?

```java
cf.exceptionally(ex -> fallbackValue);        // recover from error
cf.handle((result, ex) -> {                   // handle both cases
    if (ex != null) return "Error";
    return "OK: " + result;
});
cf.whenComplete((result, ex) -> log(result, ex)); // observe only
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What happens if you use thenApply when the function returns a CF? You get CF<CF<T>>, which is nested and requires double unwrapping.
- Can you chain multiple thenCombine calls? Yes, to combine three or more futures.
- What is the difference between thenAccept and thenApply? thenAccept takes a Consumer (no return value), thenApply takes a Function (returns value).
